---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { useTranslations } from '../../i18n';
import { sameSlugLangHrefs } from '../../utils/url';

const t = useTranslations('en');
---

<BaseLayout locale="en" title={t('nav.about')} description={t('site.tagline')} langHrefs={sameSlugLangHrefs('/about/')} alternates={sameSlugLangHrefs('/about/')}>
  <h1>{t('nav.about')}</h1>
  <p>
    sharelab tells school physics through <strong>interactive demos</strong>: drag a slider, poke the
    parameters, and watch the textbook formula actually run — projectile motion with drag, the chaotic
    double pendulum, a Doppler effect you can hear.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every article follows one rule: <strong>the numbers are verified by independent computation before
    publishing</strong>. The recurring theme is the boundary case — the place where a familiar formula is
    still correct, but its range of validity is narrower than the way we memorised it.
  </p>
  <p>
    In between are Creative Commons–licensed science articles republished from The Conversation,
    SciDev.Net, Global Voices and Mongabay, plus occasional notes from personal projects built with
    Claude Code. Most posts are in Vietnamese; every demo is plain Canvas with no external libraries,
    open source on <a href="https://github.com/angelline2005/sharelab" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
